Qore PDF module packages
========================

Runtime includes native bindings, source/AOT PdfDataProvider, compiler metadata,
provider resources and every committed translation. Separate documentation and
debug-symbol packages are built. This snapshot targets qore-testing.

Export tracked sources excluding debian/ and Files-Excluded paths, preserving
all retained third-party license notices. PoDoFo 1.0.3 and the existing CFF leak
fix are part of the tracked source; private static linkage and hidden archive
symbols prevent collision with the system PoDoFo. No bundled headers/libraries
are installed. Its graphics, font and crypto dependencies are system libraries.
No dependency is downloaded during a package build.

Prepare orig/debian sources with the changelog version, then build rootless with
dpkg-buildpackage. Native builds use the packaged Qore SDK; cross builds are
rejected. Full, -B, -A, nocheck and nodoc profiles are supported. Qualification
covers upstream suites, installed runtime/fresh qcc, source modules, translations,
metadata, debug symbols, install/purge/reinstall and reproducibility.

Only Ubuntu 26.04 amd64 / Qore SDK .12 is initially qualified. Other targets,
upgrade baselines and the Qore ABI freeze remain general-release requirements.
Private dependencies require upstream security monitoring. Testing PPA sources
must be signed and depend on the matching published SDK.

PDFium is required for the Debian/Ubuntu package. Install libpdfium-qore-dev
from the matching Qore testing repository before building. Its separate
qore-pdfium source package bootstraps pinned PDFium and GN sources offline,
uses system image/font/Unicode libraries and carries a milestone-specific
SONAME. ENABLE_PDFIUM=ON and REQUIRE_PDFIUM=ON make missing support fatal;
package tests also require rendering and text extraction to be available.

Renderer regression tests cover overflowing Qore integers, bitmap limits,
file/memory parity, concurrent calls and interrupted memory operations.
The bundled PoDoFo encryption context has one consistent opaque OpenSSL
type in all translation units, allowing a valid LTO build. Its original
CFF font-embedding leak fix is retained.
